The cleanup uses `un.then((f) => f())` without a `.catch`. If the `listen()` promise ever rejects (e.g., during rapid mount/unmount or in test environments), the cleanup will throw synchronously and React will surface it as an effect error, breaking strict-mode double-invocation. Wrap with `.catch(() => {})` (matching the pattern in `tasksRef` elsewhere) so the unlisten failure is swallowed instead of propagated.

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const un = listen(WORKFLOW_VISIBILITY_EVENT, () => setShowWorkflowTasks(getShowWorkflowTasks()));
  return () => {
    un.then((f) => f()).catch(() => {});
  };
}, []);
```

