Wasteful redundant DB lookup + unused tuple element. The wf_id resolution performs `load_workflow` twice in the `Some` branch (once to check existence, once again below to fetch `created_at`), and the `None` branch calls `load_workflow` against a freshly-minted UUID that provably doesn't exist. Also, the `let (created_at, _) = match … { … prev.name, … name.clone() }` tuple destructuring binds `prev.name` and computes `name.clone()` only to discard both — `name.clone()` allocates a `String` per new workflow for nothing, and `prev.name` triggers an unused-variable warning. Collapse into a single `load_workflow` whose result drives both existence-check and `created_at`:

```rust
let wf_id = match &input.workflow_id {
    Some(id) => id.clone(),
    None => uuid::Uuid::new_v4().simple().to_string(),
};
let prev = load_workflow(conn, &wf_id)?;
if input.workflow_id.is_some() && prev.is_none() {
    return Err(CommandError::TaskNotFound(input.workflow_id.clone().unwrap()));
}
let created_at = prev.map(|p| p.created_at).unwrap_or(Some(now));
```

